AWS Identity & Access Management (IAM) Basics

When it comes to security in AWS, everything starts with IAM (Identity and Access Management).
It controls who can access what in your AWS environment — making it one of the most crucial topics for both interviews and real-world usage.



🔹 What is IAM?

IAM enables you to securely manage access to AWS services and resources.
You can create users, groups, and roles — and assign them permissions through policies.



🔹 Core IAM Components:

🧑💻 Users – Individual identities for people or apps needing AWS access.
👥 Groups – Collections of users sharing the same permissions.
🎭 Roles – Temporary credentials assigned to entities like EC2, Lambda, or other AWS services.
📜 Policies – JSON documents that define permissions (“Allow” or “Deny”).



🔹 Best Practices:

✅ Follow the Principle of Least Privilege – Give only the permissions required.
✅ Use IAM Roles instead of embedding access keys.
✅ Enable MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) for root and IAM users.
✅ Regularly rotate Access Keys and audit permissions.


